content:
In this post, I write a type-safe `groupBy` function in TypeScript — grouping an array of items by a derived key without losing type information along the way.

## Why bother

`Array.prototype.reduce` can group items just fine, but most hand-rolled versions fall back to `Record<string, T[]>` and throw away the actual key type, so you lose autocomplete and type-checking on the result.

## The fix

```ts
function groupBy<T, K extends PropertyKey>(items: T[], key: (item: T) => K): Record<K, T[]> {
  return items.reduce((acc, item) => {
    const group = key(item);
    (acc[group] ??= []).push(item);
    return acc;
  }, {} as Record<K, T[]>);
}
```

A few gotchas worth calling out:

- Type inference breaks down once the key function returns a union wider than the actual keys present.
- `Record<K, T[]>` assumes every possible `K` value shows up, which isn't guaranteed at runtime.
- A `Partial<Record<K, T[]>>` return type is more honest, at the cost of forcing callers to handle `undefined`.
